       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. You have the authority to make clinical decisions. But 1600 mg four times daily is 6,400 mg/day, twice the maximum daily dose, and the 1600 mg single dose is also twice the maximum. I know of no indication that supports it. The risks include GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. Higher doses also add no extra analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit, because the effect plateaus.\n\nI can't see a pharmacist verification step or a hard-stop override here, so I'd be the only safeguard between this order and the patient.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a maximum-dose order now**, such as ibuprofen 800 mg by mouth 4 times daily (3,200 mg/day). I can add a note saying the dose was capped and that you'll reassess.\n- **Enter a different regimen** if the goal is stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect. Options include scheduled acetaminophen with ibuprofen, or another agent.\n- **Route the original order through pharmacy or your institution's formal high-dose override process.** If a clinical reason exists, such as a protocol or trial, that path gives it proper review.\n\nTell me which you'd like, and I'll enter it right away."
      }
